//! Generates the app's Win32 API bindings and keeps the checked-in copy
//! current. This crate has no code of its own; the work happens in
//! `tests/win32_bindings.rs`.
//!
//! `src-tauri/src/win32.rs` is generated by `windows-bindgen` from the
//! arguments in this crate's `bindings.txt` and checked in, so building the app
//! never runs the generator. The test regenerates the file and fails if the
//! result differs from the checked-in copy. That happens when someone edits
//! `bindings.txt` or `win32.rs` by hand, or when a `windows-bindgen` bump
//! changes the output.
//!
//! To regenerate `win32.rs` after any of those, run:
//!
//! ```text
//! FASTPEQ_BLESS_BINDINGS=1 cargo test -p fastpeq-win32-bindgen
//! ```
//!
//! In PowerShell, clear the variable afterwards. Otherwise every later test run
//! in that session regenerates the file instead of checking it:
//!
//! ```text
//! $env:FASTPEQ_BLESS_BINDINGS = 1; cargo test -p fastpeq-win32-bindgen; Remove-Item Env:FASTPEQ_BLESS_BINDINGS
//! ```
//!
//! Only the value `1` regenerates, and an up-to-date file is never rewritten.
//! The generator formats its output with `rustfmt`, so it needs the rustfmt
//! component installed.
